# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12 starter toolbox talks (one page each). Each: id, title, why, points, ask (discussion questions)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TALKS = [
    ('falls', 'Fall protection on roofs', 'Falls from roofs are the leading cause of death in construction — and most happen from under 20 feet. On our jobs, 6 feet is the line: at 6 feet or more you are protected.',
     ['Steep roof (over 4/12): harness + shock-absorbing lanyard or rope grab + a roof anchor installed per the instructions. Tie off **before** you step out onto the roof.',
      'Inspect your harness and lanyard every day: stitching, webbing, buckles, D-ring, the shock pack. A harness that caught a fall is done.',
      'Anchor rated 5,000 lb per person — never a vent, gutter or pipe.',
      'Keep the lifeline as short as practical; know your fall clearance so you do not hit the ground or a lower roof.',
      'Cover or guard skylights and holes. Never sit or step on a skylight.',
      'No roof work in high wind, lightning, rain, frost or ice.',
      'Rescue plan: who goes for the ladder, who calls 911. Suspension trauma starts within minutes.'],
     ['Where is our anchor going on this roof, and who installs it?', 'What is the fall clearance on the lowest edge?', 'Who calls 911 and who brings the ladder if someone falls?']),
    ('ladders', 'Ladder safety', 'Ladder falls send thousands of workers to the ER every year — usually from a ladder that was the wrong one, set up wrong, or used wrong.',
     ['Inspect before each use: rungs, rails, feet, locks. Damaged? Tag it "Do not use" and take it out of service.',
      'Extension ladder: 4-to-1 angle, level firm footing, tied off at the top, 3 feet above the landing.',
      'Stepladder: fully open and locked. Never stand on the top step or top cap.',
      'Three points of contact. Face the ladder. Tools on your belt or hoisted up.',
      'Belt buckle between the rails — move the ladder instead of reaching.',
      'Fiberglass ladders near any electrical source; stay 10 feet from power lines.',
      'One person at a time.'],
     ['Which ladder on this job needs to be tied off today?', 'What is the 4-to-1 rule for a 20-foot climb?', 'Any ladders on the truck that should be tagged out?']),
    ('scaffolds', 'Scaffolds and lifts', 'Scaffolds and lifts fail when they are built on bad footing, not fully planked, overloaded or used without guardrails.',
     ['A competent person inspects the scaffold before every shift and after storms.',
      'Base plates and mud sills — never blocks or bricks.',
      'Fully planked; guardrails at 10 feet and up; use the ladder access, not the cross-braces.',
      'Know the load rating and stay under it — material counts.',
      'Do not move a rolling scaffold with anyone on it; lock the wheels.',
      'Lifts: trained operators only; inspect first; stay inside the rails; harness in a boom lift.',
      '10 feet from power lines, always.'],
     ['Who is our competent person for this scaffold?', 'What did today\'s inspection find?', 'Where is the closest power line to our lift path?']),
    ('heat', 'Heat illness: Water. Rest. Shade.', 'Heat illness can kill — and it can hit the strongest guy on the crew, especially in the first week of hot weather or the first week back.',
     ['Drink water often — about a cup every 15–20 minutes, even if you are not thirsty. Skip energy drinks and alcohol.',
      'Rest in the shade; take the breaks — they are part of the job.',
      'New or returning workers: ease in over the first week.',
      'Do roof work early; heavy work in the cooler hours.',
      'Heat exhaustion — heavy sweat, dizziness, headache, nausea: stop, shade, cool down, water, tell the crew lead.',
      'Heat stroke — confusion, slurred speech, collapse, hot skin: **call 911**, cool them with water and ice right away.',
      'Use the buddy system. Check on each other.'],
     ['Where is the water and the shade on this job?', 'Who on the crew is new to the heat this week?', 'What would make you call 911?']),
    ('electrical', 'Electrical safety and GFCI', 'Electrocution is one of OSHA\'s "Fatal Four." On remodels and water losses the hazards are hidden in walls, ceilings and wet floors.',
     ['GFCI on every temporary cord and tool — including generator power. Push the test button first.',
      'Check cords daily: no cuts, no missing ground pins, no tape repairs. Heavy-duty outdoor cords only.',
      'Keep cords out of water and out of doorways where they get pinched.',
      'Shut off and lock/tag the circuit before demo near wiring. Assume it is live until proven dead.',
      'Flooded areas: power off before you step in the water.',
      'Overhead lines: 10 feet minimum for you, your ladder and your material.',
      'Only a licensed electrician works on the electrical system.'],
     ['Where is the panel on this job, and which breakers feed our work area?', 'Which of our cords should be thrown out today?', 'Where are the overhead lines?']),
    ('tools', 'Hand and power tools', 'Tools cause cuts, amputations, eye injuries and nail-gun wounds — almost always when a guard is missing or a shortcut is taken.',
     ['Guards stay on. Never pin back a circular-saw guard.',
      'Inspect before use; tag out damaged tools and cords.',
      'Unplug or pull the battery before changing a blade or clearing a jam.',
      'Nail guns: sequential trigger, finger off the trigger when moving, never point it at anyone, watch for nails through thin stock.',
      'Safety glasses always; face shield for grinding.',
      'Clamp small pieces; keep both hands away from the blade path.',
      'Powder-actuated tools: certified operators only.'],
     ['Which tool on this job is most likely to hurt someone, and why?', 'Are all our saw guards working?', 'Who is trained on the powder-actuated tool?']),
    ('silica', 'Silica dust', 'Cutting fiber-cement siding, concrete, brick, tile or stone makes fine silica dust that scars the lungs for life and causes cancer. You cannot see the dangerous part.',
     ['Use the control OSHA lists for the task: water on the blade, or a shroud with a HEPA vacuum.',
      'Cut fiber-cement with shears or a score-and-snap where you can, or a saw with dust collection.',
      'Wear the respirator the task requires — and only if you have been fit-tested and medically cleared for it.',
      'No dry sweeping and no compressed air on dust. HEPA vacuum or wet clean-up.',
      'Keep others (and the homeowner) away while cutting; cut outside and downwind.',
      'Wash up before eating; do not take dusty clothes home.'],
     ['Which tasks on this job make silica dust?', 'Is our vacuum a HEPA vacuum, and is the filter good?', 'Who needs a respirator for today\'s cutting?']),
    ('lead', 'Lead-safe work in older homes (RRP)', 'Homes built before 1978 may have lead paint. Dust from sanding, scraping or demolition poisons children and adults — and it goes home on your clothes.',
     ['Pre-1978 home or child-occupied facility? Assume lead unless tested.',
      'A certified renovator directs the work; the "Renovate Right" pamphlet is given to the owner before we start.',
      'Post warning signs; contain the area with plastic; keep occupants and pets out.',
      'No open-flame burning, no power sanding without a HEPA shroud, no heat gun over 1,100 °F.',
      'Mist before scraping. Clean with a HEPA vacuum and wet wipes; do the cleaning verification.',
      'Wash hands and face before eating, drinking or smoking; change clothes before getting in the truck.',
      'Suspected asbestos (old tile, mastic, popcorn ceiling, pipe wrap)? Stop and call the PM.'],
     ['When was this house built?', 'Who is our certified renovator on this job?', 'Where does our containment go?']),
    ('mold', 'Water damage and mold jobs', 'Mitigation jobs mix electrical, structural, biological and chemical hazards in one room. Slow down at the door and look first.',
     ['Power off to wet areas before you step in standing water. GFCI on all drying equipment.',
      'Look up: a bulging ceiling can come down. Drain it from the side.',
      'Know the water category: Category 2 and 3 (gray / black) means gloves, eye protection, suits, boots and the right respirator.',
      'Mold: small areas — N95, gloves, goggles; larger — containment, negative air with HEPA, and a respirator with P100 filters.',
      'Antimicrobials only as labeled — read the SDS and ventilate.',
      'Never run gas engines (generators, pumps, pressure washers) inside or near open windows — carbon monoxide.',
      'Wash before eating; clean tools and boots before leaving the job.'],
     ['What category is this water?', 'Is the power off to the wet area — who confirmed it?', 'What PPE does this room need?']),
    ('hazcom', 'Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets (HazCom)', 'Sealants, solvents, primers, foams, fuels, cleaners and antimicrobials can burn skin and eyes, damage lungs or catch fire.',
     ['Every container keeps its label. Pour it into something else? Label it, or use it up today.',
      'The SDS tells you the hazards, the PPE, first aid and what to do with a spill. They are in the truck binder and in Company documents → Safety.',
      'Learn the pictograms: flame, skull, exclamation mark, corrosion, health hazard.',
      'Wear the gloves and eye protection the SDS lists; ventilate indoors.',
      'Wet cement burns — gloves, long sleeves, and wash it off skin right away.',
      'Store flammables in approved cans away from heat; no smoking.',
      'Splash in the eyes: flush 15 minutes and call the crew lead.'],
     ['Which products on this job have a flame or corrosion pictogram?', 'Where is the SDS binder?', 'Where is the eyewash?']),
    ('housekeeping', 'Housekeeping and fire prevention', 'Trips, falls on the same level and job-site fires come from clutter, scrap and careless hot work.',
     ['Clean as you go; clear walkways, stairs and exits; full clean-up at the end of the day.',
      'Pull or bend nails in scrap right away.',
      'Debris goes down a chute or into the dumpster — never thrown from a roof without a barricaded drop zone.',
      'Extinguisher (2-A or bigger) within 100 feet, and in every truck. P-A-S-S.',
      'Hot work: clear combustibles within 35 feet, extinguisher at hand, fire watch at least 30 minutes after — check the attic and under the deck after torch work.',
      'Gas cans: approved and labeled; refuel engines when off and cool.',
      'At occupied homes: secure the site every night — ladders down or locked, holes covered, tools away.'],
     ['What is the trip hazard on this site right now?', 'Where is our extinguisher?', 'Who is the fire watch after today\'s hot work?']),
    ('ppe', 'Personal protective equipment', 'PPE is the last line of defense — but it only works if it fits, it is in good shape, and you wear it the whole task.',
     ['Hard hat where anything can fall or you can bump your head, and on every commercial site.',
      'Safety glasses with side shields for cutting, nailing, grinding, tear-off and chemicals; face shield over them for grinding.',
      'Hearing protection around saws, compressors, nailers, demolition and drying equipment in closed rooms.',
      'The right gloves: cut-resistant, chemical-resistant or leather — match the glove to the task.',
      'Boots with good tread; soft soles on roofs; safety-toe where things can crush.',
      'Hi-vis near traffic and equipment.',
      'Damaged PPE? Tell the crew lead — GrayCo replaces it.'],
     ['What PPE does each task today need?', 'Is anyone\'s PPE worn out or missing?', 'Where are we too loud without hearing protection?']),
]
